yoyo tree <bad arg> and yoyo undo <bad arg> now exit 1 instead of 0. The fix is committed as 4770bc05 with Part of #982, and #982's status block is updated.

Exit codes on the built binary, run from `/tmp`:

| command | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| tree zz_bad | 0 | 1 |
| tree /nonexistent | 0 | 1 |
| undo zz | 0 | 1 |
| undo (no argument), undo 0 | 0 | 0 |
| tree, tree 2 (inside a git repo) | 0 | 0 |

The usage line still prints to stdout and stderr stays empty. That matches the lint <unknown sub> fix, which also exits 1. The existing "not a git repo" failure in tree's handler already used 1.

Changes:
• **`src/commands_tree.rs:** tree` already had a status-returning function. Its usage branch returned 0 and now returns 1.
• **src/commands_git.rs:** `undo` had its argument parsing written inline in `handle_undo`. I moved it into one parser, `parse_undo_arg`. Both `handle_undo` (the in-session `/undo command) and the new handle_undo_status (used by yoyo undo` from the shell) read it, so the two paths can't disagree about what counts as a usage error. Both still print the same text.
• **`src/dispatch_sub.rs:** the shell's undo branch now exits through exit_if_nonzero`, like its neighbours.
• **docs/src/usage/commands.md:** added tree and undo to the exit-code paragraph. That's a fourth file beyond the task's limit of three, but leaving the docs stale would have broken the docs rule.

Tests: I added three tests that check the status code without running the exiting command, so they don't kill the test binary. The cases that must still exit 0 are pinned in the same tests. I broke each status check on purpose (one command each, one at a time): each test failed by name, and I restored them with no markers left behind. Build, the full test suite, clippy with -D warnings and fmt --check all pass.

Still exits 0 when it shouldn't:
• undo --all or undo --last-commit that runs and then fails.
• The other items on #982's list: def/outline when nothing is found, bare run, and yoyo map /nonexistent_zz. That last one prints "(no supported source files with symbols found)" and exits 0, so a missing path looks like an empty project. I ran it myself before adding it to the issue as found but not fixed.

Issue #982: gh issue edit succeeded. The "Already fixed" list now names tree and undo with the commit sha, and map <missing path> is on the "still exit 0" list.
